{#-
  robots.txt.j2

  Environment-aware robots.txt template for Chapter 15 of
  SEO for Engineers, Volume 1. Use this when robots.txt is generated
  from a template at build or request time rather than served from a
  host-based map (see serve-robots-nginx.conf for the latter).

  The defensive point of this template is the final `{% else %}`
  branch. A naive two-branch template (`production` vs everything
  else) fails *open toward Disallow: /* whenever the environment
  variable is unset, misspelled, or not populated on the CI agent.
  This template instead enumerates the known non-production
  environments explicitly and raises on anything unrecognized, so a
  misconfiguration fails the build loudly instead of silently
  shipping the wrong file.

  NOTE: `fail` is NOT a built-in Jinja2 function. Vanilla Jinja2 has
  no way to raise from a template. Register it as a global on the
  environment before rendering, e.g.:

      def _raise(message):
          raise RuntimeError(message)

      env = jinja2.Environment(undefined=jinja2.StrictUndefined)
      env.globals["fail"] = _raise

  Render with the `environment` variable set, e.g.:

      env.get_template("robots.txt.j2").render(environment="production")

  Reference: SEO for Engineers, Volume 1, Chapter 15.
-#}
{% if environment == "production" %}
User-agent: *
Allow: /
Sitemap: https://www.example.com/sitemap.xml
{% elif environment in ["staging", "preview", "dev"] %}
User-agent: *
Disallow: /
{% else %}
{{ fail("robots.txt template rendered without a known environment: " + environment) }}
{% endif %}
